# `dict.fromkeys("ab")` SEGFAULTED. The stdlib call site builds this call BY
# NAME and cannot resolve overloads by type, so a str went straight into a
# TPyList parameter and the callee dereferenced a string as an object.
#
# This is the population named in project_nilpy_byname_findproc_lowerings_are_
# the_unchecked_population: every by-name lowering that lands on a pylib routine
# with a TPyList parameter is one "wrong argument type = SIGSEGV" away. The cure
# is the same each time — take a VARIANT and go through pylist_v, the one bridge
# that turns any Python iterable into a list — not a check at the call site,
# which would have to be repeated per site.
#
# `list(dict.fromkeys(xs))` is the standard order-preserving dedupe, so the str
# case is not exotic: dedupe the characters of a word and you are here.
# bug-nilpy-dict-fromkeys-of-a-str-segfaults
